Work that is understood well enough to write down but has not been done.
Entries are dated and say what was established, so that picking one up does not
mean starting the investigation over.

Not a backlog of ideas -- something belongs here once the shape of the problem
is known and the open questions are named.


================================================================================
Read p'(psi) and FF'(psi) from NetCDF, including MaNTA output
Raised 2026-08-24.
================================================================================

  DECIDED 2026-09-01, AND THE SCHEMA IS ALREADY RESERVED FOR IT. The
  configuration layer now recognises `[source] ProfileFile` and the per-profile
  `<Key>Variable` and `<Key>Fit` keys and refuses them with a message saying
  they are not implemented, rather than as unknown keys. So adding this is
  purely additive: change the refusals into reads. Two things were settled at
  the same time.

  THE MISSING-DERIVATIVE RULE. A NetCDF variable carrying VALUES BUT NO
  DERIVATIVE is refused unless the configuration opts in per profile, with e.g.
  `TemperatureFit = "pchip"`. meq::Profile's contract is that prime() is the
  exact derivative of operator(), and a fitted derivative is a modelling
  choice: a monotone fit and a natural spline disagree about whether an edge
  pedestal overshoots into a negative density. That choice belongs to whoever
  knows what the file means, recorded in the file that records the run -- not
  to a default buried in a reader.

  AND THE DERIVATIVE SHIFT BELOW DOES NOT ARISE FOR THE ROTATING SOURCE, which
  changes which source this work should be built against. MaNTA writes one group
  per variable, each carrying u(t,x) "Value" and q(t,x) "Derivative" over a
  shared x. That (x, u, q) triple is an (abscissa, value, derivative) triple,
  which is meq::Knot, so a SplineProfile drops straight out and doublePrime()
  comes from the cubic. The shift is a property of meq::MHDSource's PRODUCT
  parameterisation -- it stores p' and gg', so it wants (p', p'') and MaNTA
  gives (p, p') -- and meq::RotatingSource takes T_s and n_s0 THEMSELVES, so
  (u, q) is exactly what it needs. Build this against the rotating source first;
  the MHD one is the awkward case, not the easy one.

  BUT READ THE NEXT THREE PARAGRAPHS BEFORE BELIEVING THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY.
  An earlier version of this entry said the triple came straight off the file.
  It comes off a RESAMPLING of the file's real content, and the file is not
  only profiles.

  MaNTA's PROFILES ARE NODAL FINITE ELEMENTS, AND x IS AN OUTPUT GRID, NOT THE
  NODES. SystemSolver::initialiseNetCDF builds `gridpoints` as nOut points
  UNIFORMLY spaced over [lowerBoundary, upperBoundary] and calls
  NetCDFIO::SetOutputGrid on it; AddVariable then evaluates the DGSoln onto
  that grid. So u and q are the FE solution and its derivative SAMPLED, and the
  fidelity of what meq would read is set by nOut -- a run-time output choice --
  rather than by MaNTA's polynomial order. A Hermite cubic through those samples
  is an interpolant of an interpolant. That is fine at large nOut and is a
  measurement somebody should make rather than assume; it also means the Grid
  group's CellBoundaries and PolyOrder are the things to read if the FE
  representation itself is ever wanted.

  THE FILE CONTAINS MORE THAN PROFILES. TransportSystem has virtual
  initialiseDiagnostics / writeDiagnostics / finaliseDiagnostics hooks, and
  NetCDFIO offers AddGroup, AddVariable, AddScalarVariable and AddTimeSeries, so
  a physics case writes whatever it likes into the same file alongside the
  solution. A reader must SELECT BY NAME and must not assume that every group is
  a profile or that every variable over x is one. That is the direct argument
  for the schema already reserved here: a per-profile <Key>Variable naming its
  source, rather than any positional or structural convention.

  AND THE GROUP NAMES ARE PHYSICAL IN A REAL RUN. AddVariable is called with
  problem->getVariableName(i), so a production file has meaningful group names;
  the Var0, Var1, ... seen in ../MaNTA/algderiv_*.nc are test cases that do not
  name their variables. Do not design around Var<i>.

  One thing still open: whether MaNTA's x is psi, normalised Psi, or a radial
  coordinate needing a chain rule -- q is du/dx, so if x is not psi then the
  derivative needs the chain rule and the whole (u, q) convenience above is
  conditional on that. The abscissa variable should be named in the
  configuration rather than assumed, and its units checked.

  Today `meq::SplineProfile` reads one format: a text table of
  `psi  f(psi)  f'(psi)` triples, via `fromFile()` / `fromStream()`
  (src/meq/Profiles.cpp). Configuration reaches it through `[source]` with
  `SourceType = "mhd"` and the `PPrimeFile` / `GGPrimeFile` keys.

  Wanted: the same two profiles read from a NetCDF file, which may or may not
  have been produced by MaNTA (../MaNTA). So there are really two readers, or
  one reader with a configurable name mapping.

  WHAT THE MaNTA FORMAT ACTUALLY IS

  Established by reading ../MaNTA/NetCDFIO.{hpp,cpp} and dumping
  ../MaNTA/python/Tests/JAXAuxTest.ref.nc. It uses the netCDF C++4 API
  (`#include <netcdf>`, netCDF::NcFile). Layout:

      dimensions:  t = UNLIMITED          time
                   x = <n>                space
      variables:   double t(t)            "Time since start of simulation", s
                   double x(x)            the spatial coordinate
                   double nVariables      count, at root
      group Grid:  CellBoundaries(Index), PolyOrder
      group VarN:  u(t,x)     "Value"
                   q(t,x)     "Derivative"
                   sigma(t,x) "Flux"

  So each variable is a group named `Var0`, `Var1`, ... and each carries BOTH
  the value and its derivative on the `x` grid. That is promising, because a
  Hermite cubic wants exactly a value and a derivative per knot -- `meq::Knot`
  is `{psi, value, derivative}`, which is the same triple.

  THE SHAPE TO BUILD, DECIDED 2026-09-01. Four kinds of file, all of which look
  like a filename to the user, and the design goal is that meq works out which
  it is rather than making the author declare it.

    (1) full MaNTA output, which also carries the species set, and eventually a
        direct FE representation rather than the resampled grid.
        *** DEFERRED until that file shape is specified. *** The FE output does
        not exist on the MaNTA side yet, and "load the whole species set" needs
        the layout pinned before it can mean anything. Nothing below depends on
        it; it slots in as one more branch of step 2.
    (2) a NetCDF holding a profile f(x), with x either raw psi or normalised.
    (3) a NetCDF holding f(x, y1, ..., yn) which becomes (2) once the other
        axes are fixed.
    (4) either of those, with the derivative supplied as well, on the same
        abscissa. (Assume the same abscissa; a file that disagrees with itself
        about that is a file to refuse, not to interpolate.)

  THE UNIFYING IDEA is that all four are "a NetCDF file, plus however much meq
  can work out for itself", so the reader is a RESOLUTION PIPELINE and not four
  readers:

    a. A path ending .nc opens as NetCDF, and MAY CARRY THE VARIABLE WITH IT:
       "prof.nc/T_i" addresses T_i inside prof.nc. Resolution is by LONGEST
       EXISTING-FILE PREFIX -- stat each candidate split and take the first
       that is a regular file; the remainder is the in-file path. NOT by
       splitting at the last slash: NCZarr datasets are DIRECTORIES and this
       netCDF has NCZarr on, so "data.zarr/T_i" may be a real filesystem path.
    b. Find the VALUE variable: the in-file part of (a) if the path carried
       one; else the config's <Key>Variable; else, if the file offers exactly
       one plausible candidate, take it and say so.
    c. Find the ABSCISSA: the variable's dimension that carries a coordinate
       variable, which is the netCDF convention and costs the author nothing.
    d. Fix any REMAINING AXES -- case (3). A length-1 axis is squeezed. A
       time-like axis defaults to its LAST index. Anything else the config must
       pin, and it may do so TWO WAYS: DAP-style brackets inside the address,
       "prof.nc/T_i[40]", positional exactly as DAP defines them; or an
       explicit <Key>Slice table keyed BY DIMENSION NAME,
       { time = 40, species = 0 }. Named beats positional wherever the file
       names its dimensions, because it survives a reordering that brackets do
       not -- so Slice is the one to reach for and the brackets are the terse
       standard-shaped alternative. Naming one axis both ways is a REFUSAL, not
       a precedence rule. Until an axis is pinned the failure names it and its
       length rather than guessing.
    e. Find the DERIVATIVE -- case (4): the config's name, a small set of
       naming conventions, or an attribute pointing at it. Failing all three,
       REFUSE unless <Key>Fit opts in, per the rule already settled above.

  WHY "foo.nc/var" AND NOT SOMETHING STANDARD: THERE IS NO STANDARD FOR IT, AND
  THAT WAS TESTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. On this machine -- netCDF 4.9.3, with
  DAP2, DAP4 and NCZarr all built in -- `ncdump file.nc/T_i` fails with "Not a
  directory" and `ncks file.nc/T_i` with "file not found". No netCDF tool
  accepts the concatenation. What IS standard is the second half: netCDF-4
  group paths are slash-separated, and `ncdump -v /profiles/T_e` and
  `-v profiles/T_e` both work. So "foo.nc/var" invents only the JOIN and
  inherits the in-file address -- which is why group support costs nothing,
  "prof.nc/profiles/T_e" splitting into a file plus exactly what ncdump -v
  takes. The OS refusing to walk into a file is what makes the split
  unambiguous, and it is why the rule is a stat rather than a string operation.

  The one-string conventions that DO exist were considered and not adopted
  wholesale. GDAL spells it NETCDF:"file.nc":var -- real, widely used, ugly,
  and geospatial rather than general. OPeNDAP constraint expressions,
  dataset?var[0:10], are a genuine published standard that this netCDF already
  parses inside nc_open, and that is where the BRACKETS come from: meq borrows
  DAP's slice syntax rather than inventing one, and does not borrow its '?'
  separator, which would buy nothing over '/'.

  THE SAFETY MECHANISM IS ANNOUNCEMENT, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES INFERENCE
  ACCEPTABLE AT ALL. Every inference above is a place where a wrong guess gives
  a converged solution to a different problem, which is this project's standing
  worst case. So the reader prints ONE LINE PER PROFILE saying what it decided:

    meq: T_i from equilibrium.nc: variable 'T_i', 129 knots on RAW psi in
         [0, 0.42] Wb/rad, derivative from 'dT_i_dpsi', time index 40 of 41

  A user who reads that sees a wrong inference immediately; one who is never
  told cannot. This is the difference between "no burden on the author" and "no
  way to tell what happened", and it is the whole reason inference is allowed
  here when it is refused elsewhere in this codebase.

  THE ONE GENUINELY DANGEROUS INFERENCE IS RAW psi AGAINST NORMALISED psi,
  because getting it wrong rescales every profile silently and the solve still
  converges. Three signals, in order: the abscissa's `units` attribute
  (Wb/rad and its spellings mean raw; dimensionless or "1" means normalised);
  failing that, its NAME; failing that, its RANGE, where [0, 1] is strong
  evidence and anything else is not. Announce the conclusion, always, and let
  the configuration override it -- and treat "no signal at all" as a refusal
  rather than a default, because there is no safe default here.

  WHAT "NO EXTRA BURDEN" MEANS CONCRETELY, and it is the acceptance test for
  this design: a well-formed one-dimensional file needs `TemperatureFile =
  "x.nc"` and nothing else -- no variable name, no abscissa, no units, no slice.
  Case (3) costs one key naming the slice. Case (4) costs nothing and works
  better. If a plain case (2) file needs more than a filename, the inference is
  not finished.

  A REQUEST ON THE MaNTA SIDE falls out of (1) and is not meq's to write: the
  ability to output the FE representation -- coefficients, cell boundaries and
  polynomial order -- rather than only the uniform resampling SetOutputGrid
  produces. meq would then read MaNTA's actual solution instead of an
  interpolant of a sampling of it, and case (1) could stop being a resampling
  at all.

  RAISE IT WITH MaNTA RATHER THAN EDITING MANTA-COUPLING.md, which is a RECEIVED
  document: its own header says it is "written from MaNTA's side" and that the
  authoritative copy is FieldModel.hpp, FieldModelSpec.hpp and
  docs/field_coupling.rst in that tree, so anything meq writes into it is a
  local edit to somebody else's contract. This is the same rule as
  ../mfem-hdg-dev being receive-only, for the same reason.

  THE FOUR QUESTIONS TO SETTLE BEFORE WRITING ANY OF IT

  1. It is a derivative SHIFT, not a match. MaNTA gives (f, df/dx). meq's
     `MHDSource` wants p' and gg' as profiles OF psi, and calls `prime()` on
     them for the Newton Jacobian -- so it needs (p', p'') and (gg', gg'').
     If MaNTA's stored variable is p, then its (u, q) gives (p, dp/dx), which is
     one derivative short of what meq needs. Options, in rough order of
     preference:
       (a) have the MaNTA physics case output p' directly as its own variable,
           so u = p' and q = p'' and the triple lines up exactly;
       (b) read (p, dp/dx) and differentiate the spline once more -- loses an
           order and makes the Jacobian noisier than the residual, which is
           precisely the failure mode that shows up as Newton converging
           linearly rather than quadratically;
       (c) read only values and fit a spline, discarding MaNTA's derivatives.
     (a) is much the best if the MaNTA side can be arranged. Decide this first;
     it determines everything else.

     PARTLY ANSWERED, 2026-09-01, AT THE TOP OF THIS ENTRY: the shift is a
     property of meq::MHDSource's PRODUCT parameterisation, and
     meq::RotatingSource takes T_s and n_s0 THEMSELVES, so MaNTA's (u, q) is
     exactly what it needs and (a) is unnecessary there. Build this against the
     rotating source and the question only arises for the MHD one.

  2. What is `x`, and how does it map to psi in [0,1]? meq's profiles are
     normalised to psi in [0,1] and `SplineProfile` clamps outside that. MaNTA's
     `x` is a radial coordinate whose meaning is set by the physics case, and
     nothing in the file says which. Needs an explicit config key naming the
     convention, plus the normalisation, rather than a guess. If x is not psi
     the chain rule lands on top of question 1.

     ADDED 2026-08-29: the consumer for a profile in normalised flux now exists.
     `meq::NormalisedMHDSource` takes dp/dPsi and (g dg/dPsi) as functions of
     Psi = psi/psi_ax, and the solver carries psi_ax as an unknown rather than
     making the caller pick one -- so a table read in Psi has a correct home and
     does not need denormalising on the way in. That removes one of the wrong
     answers this question used to have. It does NOT answer the question: what x
     means is still MaNTA's to say.

  3. Which group is which? CORRECTED 2026-08-25 by MANTA-COUPLING.md, written
     from the MaNTA side: the premise below was out of date, and the reference
     file it was established from is one of the three exceptions.

     MaNTA physics cases name their own variables now, and the netCDF group
     takes that name -- so a modern case writes groups called `n`, `p_i`, `T_e`,
     each with a `description` attribute and `units` on every variable inside.
     `Var0`/`Var1` survive only for the three cases whose width comes from
     config and which therefore have no names to give (MatrixDiffusion,
     MatrixDiffusionTest, LinearDiffSourceTest) -- and JAXAuxTest.ref.nc, which
     this entry was written from, is one of them.

     A config key naming the mapping is still needed, since a group called `p_i`
     still has to be declared to be the pressure meq wants. But the information
     is there to match against rather than absent, and the `units` attribute is
     a free consistency check worth asserting on.

  4. Which time slice? MaNTA is time dependent and meq wants one equilibrium.
     Default to the last slice, `t` index size-1, but make it configurable and
     record the value of `t` that was used in meq's own output -- an equilibrium
     computed from an unrecorded time slice is not reproducible.

  THE NON-MaNTA CASE

  "May or may not have come from MaNTA" means there must be a flat convention
  too: 1-D variables over a single dimension, something like `psi`, `pprime`,
  `ffprime`, with derivatives optional. Detect the MaNTA layout by structure --
  the `Grid` group, or `nVariables` at root -- and fall back to the flat one.
  Prefer sniffing over a config flag, but make the choice overridable, and say
  in the log which was used.

  MECHANICS

  - `libnetcdf_c++4` is installed (/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libnetcdf_c++4.so)
    along with the C library. MaNTA links it, so the CMake pattern is there.
  - MFEM's own MFEM_USE_NETCDF is NO in ../mfem-hdg-dev, which does not matter:
    meq would link netcdf directly. The pre-modernisation Makefile did exactly
    that (`-lnetcdf_c++4 -lnetcdf`).
  - The old tree had a NetCDFIO.hpp, deleted in the restructure. It is not worth
    recovering -- it was a class declaration missing its terminating semicolon,
    so it had never been compiled. `git show v0-legacy:NetCDFIO.hpp` if curious.
  - Keep the reader out of `Profiles.{hpp,cpp}`. Those two units are
    deliberately free of every dependency including MFEM, which is what lets
    them be unit tested on their own. A `ProfilesNetCDF.{hpp,cpp}` returning a
    `SplineProfile` keeps that property.

  TESTS

  Write a small NetCDF file in the test itself rather than committing a fixture,
  so the expected values are visible next to the assertions. Cover: the MaNTA
  layout, the flat layout, a missing group, a missing variable, a time index out
  of range, and non-monotonic `x`. And check the round trip against the text
  reader -- the same profile through both paths must agree.


================================================================================
Sonic toroidal rotation
Raised 2026-08-25. SUPERSEDED 2026-09-01 by FLOW-PLAN.md, which is the design.
================================================================================

  DONE, FL-0 to FL-8, and reachable from a TOML file. src/meq/RotatingSource.*
  is the source, FLOW-PLAN.md the derivation and the findings, and CLAUDE.md's
  "Toroidal flow" every measurement. The equation is refs/RotatingGK.pdf (136)
  closed by its (96) and (97); the inputs are T_s(psi), omega(psi), n-1 density
  flux functions and I(psi). Taking q(psi) instead and finding I(psi) from it is
  ROADMAP.md item 10, not this.

  THIS ENTRY'S TWO FINDINGS ARE KEPT THERE. <phi_0>_psi = 0 is a GAUGE CHOICE
  and not a closure -- RoPP says so itself at (59), "we can add any function of
  psi to it" -- so it can be replaced by anything convenient, and the plan takes
  the local phi_0(r_ref) = 0, which keeps F a pointwise function of (r, z, psi).
  And (97)'s root is unique and bracketable, its derivative carrying Z_s^2 and
  so being strictly negative, which is what makes a safeguarded scalar Newton
  unable to fail.

  THREE THINGS THE PLAN ADDS that this entry did not have. (136) collapses to
  F = mu0 r^2 dp/dpsi|_r + g g', because the dphi_0/dpsi terms cancel against
  quasineutrality -- so the residual needs phi_0 but never its derivative, and
  only the Jacobian does. Two species need no root find at all, (97) being
  linear in phi_0 after taking logarithms. And the published rotating benchmarks
  do not all solve the same equation: Li & Zhu's rotating Solov'ev is (136)'s
  isothermal closure, Maschke-Perrin's is adiabatic and is not, and FLOW solves
  poloidal flow as well.

  ONE THING IT LEFT OPEN. No published rotating benchmark exercises the C'(psi)
  term -- Li & Zhu's Solov'ev case holds T and Omega constant and Maschke-Perrin's
  (4.7) FORCES C constant -- and that is precisely the term Li & Zhu got the sign
  of wrong. Only RotatingSourceTests' dFdPsi sweep touches it. Putting
  Maschke-Perrin into tests/analytic/ is the cheap follow-up and is not done; see
  FLOW-PLAN.md section 6.3, which verified it by substitution to 8e-26.

  Nothing else that stood here is worth keeping; git has the working.
================================================================================
Anisotropic pressure
Raised 2026-08-25.
================================================================================

  The other generalisation, and independent of the rotation one above: p_par is
  not equal to p_perp, so the scalar pressure p(psi) in the source is replaced
  by a pair, and the Grad-Shafranov equation changes shape rather than just
  gaining terms.

  NO REFERENCE IS PINNED FOR THIS YET, and one is needed before anything is
  written -- unlike the rotation case above, where refs/RotatingGK.pdf eq (136)
  is the equation and FLOW-PLAN.md is the design. The seeds below come from the
  CEDRES++ paper's own
  list of what it plans to add (refs/CEDRES.pdf, section 1 and its
  bibliography), so they are the set a production free-boundary code considered
  worth citing, not a literature search:

    Grad 1967                Toroidal containment of a plasma, Phys. Fluids 10, 137
    Maschke & Perrin 1984    An analytic solution of the stationary MHD equations
                             for a rotating toroidal plasma, Phys. Lett. A 102, 106
                             -- WRONG CITATION, and the paper in refs/ is the
                             right one: Plasma Physics 22 (1980) 579, as
                             MaschkePerrin.pdf. Its section 4 IS a benchmark for
                             the rotation work, verified by substitution to
                             8e-26, and is NOT yet in tests/analytic/; its
                             section 3, the actual polytrope, is not a benchmark
                             for it at all. See FLOW-PLAN.md section 6.3
    Goedbloed & Lifschitz 1997   Stationary symmetric MHD flows, Phys. Plasmas 4, 3544
    Zwingmann, Eriksson & Stubberfield 2001   Equilibrium analysis of tokamak
                             discharges with anisotropic pressure, PPCF 43, 1441
    Guazzotto, Betti, Manickam & Kaye 2004    Numerical study of tokamak
                             equilibria with arbitrary flow, Phys. Plasmas 11, 604
                             -- fetched, and in refs/Refs.md as
                             GuazzottoFLOW.pdf. Poloidal AND toroidal flow, so a
                             different and harder problem than (136)
    Cooper et al 2009        Three-dimensional anisotropic pressure free boundary
                             equilibria, Comput. Phys. Commun. 180, 1524
    Pustovitov 2010          Anisotropic pressure effects on plasma equilibrium in
                             toroidal systems, PPCF 52, 065001
    Fitzgerald, Appel & Hole 2013   EFIT tokamak equilibria with toroidal flow and
                             anisotropic pressure using the two-temperature
                             guiding-centre plasma, Nucl. Fusion 53, 113408

  Verify each doi against Crossref before adding any of these to refs/Refs.md --
  they are transcribed from another paper's bibliography, which is exactly the
  provenance that file's opening rule exists for. Only the Guazzotto entry has
  been checked and fetched; the rest have NOT.

  Two things to establish when a reference is chosen:

  - Whether the anisotropy enters as a modified source with the same Delta*
    operator on the left, in which case meq's discretisation is untouched and
    only Source changes; or whether it changes the operator, in which case the
    HDG assembly changes too and this is a much larger job. Fitzgerald et al
    is the likely place to look, being an implementation.
  - Whether it composes with rotation. Fitzgerald's title suggests the two are
    treated together, which would make it the reference for both entries; if so
    the two should be planned as one piece of work rather than two.


================================================================================
Couple to MaNTA as a FieldModel
Raised 2026-08-25. See MANTA-COUPLING.md, which is the specification.
================================================================================

  A second, much tighter coupling than the NetCDF entry above: meq runs
  in-process as a plugin MaNTA dlopens, its Grad-Shafranov rows join MaNTA's
  DAE, and the equilibrium and the transport are solved together by one Newton
  rather than by an outer Picard loop.

  MANTA-COUPLING.md, written from the MaNTA side, is the contract and is far
  more detailed than this entry should duplicate. What belongs here is what meq
  must change, and the two things already established.

  THE ARCHITECTURAL DEMAND, which is not a detail

  MaNTA does not want a converged equilibrium. It wants the residual rows,
  evaluated at states that are nowhere near an equilibrium, on every residual
  evaluation, without complaint. meq's Newton is subsumed into MaNTA's.

  So GradShafranovSolver needs a residual-evaluation path beside its solve()
  path: given psi and a transport state, return R without solving anything.
  Stage 4 has most of the pieces -- prepare() already assembles and reduces
  without solving, and reducedOperator()/reducedRhs() already exist because the
  Jacobian check needed them. What is missing is the framing: a standalone
  solver needs to converge, a coupled one needs to be differentiable and
  evaluable OFF the solution manifold.

  The good news is that solveB, the operation MaNTA needs and will not accept a
  dense matrix for, is exactly meq's existing hybridized solve. That is the
  natural fit and the reason the trace space is the natural DOF vector.

  The two genuinely hard items, per that note's own checklist, are
  dGeometry_dpsi INCLUDING the moving-surface term -- a flux-surface average
  differentiated with respect to a psi that moves the surface -- and making the
  pointwise Geometry hook fast enough, since MaNTA calls it once per physics
  node per residual evaluation and hands over the whole psi vector each time.

  BOTH NOW HAVE MACHINERY BEHIND THEM, WHICH IS WHAT INVERSION-PLAN.md WAS FOR.
  IN-2 builds the flux-surface averages as ONE primitive over a callable
  integrand, so dGeometry_dpsi is differentiated once rather than per slot, and
  IN-3's fit supplies the surface derivative. What is still missing on meq's side
  is IN-6's per-psi cache -- INVERSION-PLAN.md section 11.1 argues it is a
  REQUIREMENT of the pointwise call pattern rather than an optimisation, and its
  invalidation contract is MANTA-COUPLING.md section 8 -- and IN-P, the cost
  measurement, which is under way. Do not design the coupling against the
  illustrative geometry slot list: MANTA-COUPLING.md says itself that the list
  "is negotiated with the transport physics case, not fixed by MaNTA", and its
  tokamak set is known to be under revision.

  AND THE CONSUMER'S JACOBIAN HAS THIS PROJECT'S OWN FAILURE MODE. MaNTA never
  assembles its Jacobian, so a missing surface-motion term "does not produce a
  wrong answer, only slow Newton convergence". That is CLAUDE.md's "A wrong
  Jacobian is invisible to a convergence table" arriving from the consumer's
  side, and the remedy is the one meq already uses on dFdPsi: finite-difference
  dGeometry_dpsi against Geometry directly, as a unit test, and never take
  convergence of the coupled solve as evidence.

  THE BUILD FLAGS, checked 2026-08-25, and narrower than the note fears

  MANTA-COUPLING.md warns that a plugin must be compiled with the flags
  `pkg-config --cflags manta` reports -- MaNTA's MANTA_ABI_FLAGS, currently
  `-DEIGEN_USE_BLAS -march=<concrete arch>`, znver2 on this machine -- because
  Eigen aligns to the widest vector unit the compiler knows about and inlines
  expression templates into both sides of the boundary. It flags reconciling
  this with meq's CMake and with how MFEM was built as an afternoon's work.

  It is smaller than that here, because NEITHER MFEM NOR meq USES EIGEN AT ALL
  (grepped, both). Eigen appears only where MaNTA's types cross the interface,
  which is the FieldModel subclass itself. So:

    - the plugin shim needs MaNTA's ABI flags;
    - meq_core and MFEM do not, and need not be rebuilt;
    - the boundary to keep Eigen on one side of is the shim, which is a
      handful of translation units rather than the whole library.

  The other trap in that section is a real one and costs nothing to respect:
  the plugin must NOT link -lmanta. MaNTA links its core objects directly, so a
  plugin pulling in libmanta.so gets a second copy of the registry and
  registers into a map the solver never reads, silently. Compile against the
  installed headers alone.

  BLOCKED ON THE MaNTA SIDE, so do not plan around them:

    - nScalars > 0 alongside a field model is refused today. A total-current
      constraint or a plasma-current control loop would need that unblocked.
    - DegreeAdaptation with a field model is refused.
    - An objective reading geometry directly loses its dG/dpsi term in the
      adjoint.

  AND FOUR QUESTIONS THAT NOTE LEAVES OPEN, two of which are ours to answer:

    - Who owns the p(psi) mapping, the MaNTA physics case or the meq field
      model? This decides which side owns which half of the A_2 chain rule.
      Ours to negotiate.
    - Should Geometry gain a batched form? Ours to ask for; the note suspects
      the pointwise-only signature is an oversight rather than a decision, and
      it is the hook that will hurt.
    - Whether the error test needs to exclude the field block, and whether
      nScalars needs unblocking first, are MaNTA-side.


================================================================================
More nonlinear test cases
Raised 2026-08-25. A survey, not yet a decision.
================================================================================

  MOSTLY DONE -- updated 2026-08-29. When this was written the ladder was
  constant / linear / nonlinear in psi (Soloviev, McCarthy, Example 5) and only
  the last was nonlinear. Items 1 and the exponential half of item 2 have since
  been built and are in the suite; what remains is item 2's elliptic case and
  items 3 and 4, which were reading rather than work when this was written and
  still are. The survey is kept because the reasoning behind the order is what
  makes picking one up cheap.

  1. ~~FOUR NONLINEAR SOURCES WE ALREADY OWN~~ -- DONE. refs/HDG-GradShafranov-
     Adaptive.pdf sections 4.2 to 4.5 are tests/analytic/PressurePedestal.hpp
     (which carries 4.2, and CurrentHole and InternalLayer for 4.4 and 4.5) and
     TransportBarrier.hpp for 4.3. PedestalConvergence.cpp drives them, and all
     four give dF/dpsi in closed form, so the Newton Jacobian stays exact. The
     transcribed source expressions that stood here are in those fixtures now.

     AND THE PREMISE -- "stiff" -- TURNED OUT TO BE WRONG FOR THREE OF THE FOUR,
     which is the single most useful thing this item produced. 4.2, 4.3 and 4.5
     are ordinary problems on a resolved mesh, 7 to 17 raw Newton iterations,
     and both h- and p-refinement cure them independently; the "stiff sources"
     story came from benchmarking at one under-resolved point. 4.4 is the
     exception and is genuinely multi-valued -- dF/dpsi spans [-580, +566]
     against a first Dirichlet eigenvalue of 22.3. See CLAUDE.md, "Why meq's
     Newton struggles", and item 7 of ROADMAP.md.

  2. GENUINELY NONLINEAR EXACT SOLUTIONS: Kaltsas & Throumoulopoulos,
     Phys. Lett. A 380 (2016) 3373, doi 10.1016/j.physleta.2016.08.011,
     arXiv:1605.01538, OPEN ACCESS.

     Similarity reduction of the GS equation, giving closed forms for nonlinear
     free functions. The two that matter:

       f(u) = f0 + f1 u^2   -> a Weierstrass elliptic function (their eq 21)
       f(u) = f0 exp(n u)   -> sech^2 (their eq 22), plus a second class (23)

     ~~The exponential/sech^2 case~~ -- DONE, as
     tests/analytic/SimilarityExponential.hpp. It earns its rung by being exact
     where ManufacturedNonlinear.hpp is reverse-engineered from a chosen answer.

     THE WEIERSTRASS CASE, f(u) = f0 + f1 u^2 (their eq 21), IS STILL OPEN and is
     what is left of this item. The two caveats that applied to the exponential
     case apply to it as well: these are similarity solutions in a combined
     variable of the form (a r^2 +- 2 b z + const), so the domain is not free --
     one has to find a closed level set to pose the problem on, exactly the
     difficulty the Solov'ev separatrix gave -- and the paper says itself that
     for some of its reductions solutions "which possibly could result in closed
     magnetic surfaces are ruled out". Check that a usable closed contour exists
     BEFORE investing in the implementation. Whether a Weierstrass elliptic
     function is worth the dependency it drags in is the other open question, and
     the exponential case already covers "exact and genuinely nonlinear".

  3. NOT NONLINEAR, despite appearances: Atanasiu, Gunter, Lackner & Miron,
     Phys. Plasmas 11 (2004) 3510, doi 10.1063/1.1756167. Two families of exact
     solutions, chosen so that "the GS equation becomes a LINEAR inhomogeneous
     differential equation" -- so this is McCarthy's rung, not a new one. Worth
     having anyway for a different reason: it constructs X-points by
     superposition and is explicitly intended as a benchmark near X-points,
     which is where stage 5's extension technique gives out. Two free parameters
     each in the pressure and poloidal current profiles.

  4. FOR CROSS-COMPARISON RATHER THAN AS TEST CASES:
       Pataki, Cerfon, Freidberg, Greengard & O'Neil, JCP 243 (2013) 28,
         doi 10.1016/j.jcp.2013.02.045 -- a fast high-order solver.
       Palha, Koren & Felici, JCP 316 (2016) 63, doi 10.1016/j.jcp.2016.04.002 --
         mimetic spectral elements; reports convergence for Soloviev, FRC and
         spheromak, so its tables are directly comparable with ours.
       VEQ, arXiv:2606.11821 (2026) -- fixed-boundary with flexible source
         profiles, and reports both a projected residual norm and a pointwise
         strong-form residual. That pair of diagnostics is worth stealing
         regardless of whether the solver is.

  All dois above are checked against Crossref. The arXiv ids are not.

  SUGGESTED ORDER, as it now stands. (1) and the exponential half of (2) are
  done. What is left is (3), the Atanasiu X-point benchmark, which is the most
  valuable of the three BECAUSE it is near X-points -- that is where stage 5's
  extension technique gives out, and ExtensionConvergence currently dodges the
  question by posing Gamma on the interior surface psi = -0.03 rather than on the
  separatrix. Then the Weierstrass case if the contour question answers well.
  (4) is reading.

  ONE CASE THAT WAS NOT IN THIS SURVEY AND HAS SINCE BEEN BUILT: a profile
  equilibrium in NORMALISED flux, tests/analytic/HighBetaPoloidal.hpp, from
  refs/GourdainContour.pdf eq (39). It is a different axis from everything above
  -- not "how nonlinear is F in psi" but "psi_ax is a functional of the solution"
  -- and it is what drove the bordered Newton. See ROADMAP.md.


================================================================================
PARDISO: settle reproducibility
Raised 2026-08-25 as "try PARDISO again against a real oneMKL build". THAT PART
IS DONE, and so is the run-time switch. Rewritten 2026-09-01 to what is left.
================================================================================

  WHAT IS LEFT IS ONE QUESTION. PARDISO's advantage IS the threading, and
  threaded reduction order is the same non-determinism that decides the pedestal
  knife edge -- see CLAUDE.md under "On SUNDIALS", where MFEM_USE_LAPACK tips a
  marginal Newton by changing the rounding and nothing else. This suite asserts
  Newton iteration counts. iparm[33], conditional numerical reproducibility,
  exists for exactly this; it costs some of the speed back, and the question is
  how much. Measure it, do not assume it.

  WHAT IS ANSWERED, and recorded in CLAUDE.md rather than here: PARDISO runs at
  every size against oneAPI MKL 2026.1 and agrees with UMFPACK to round-off, so
  the n ~ 3000 ceiling was Debian's intel-mkl 2020.4.304 and nothing about the
  method; setTraceSolver() picks UMFPack, Pardiso or CuDSS at run time, with
  UMFPack still the default because oneMKL's licence is not everybody's to
  accept; SetReuseSymbolic() is on; and the link line no longer straddles two
  MKL installations, because meq builds its own SuiteSparse.

  DO NOT QUOTE THE TIMINGS THIS ENTRY USED TO CARRY. They were taken before
  2026-08-30, when a stray libmkl_sequential made MKL resolve sequential for
  everything, so they were sequential-against-sequential and were never labelled
  as such. The live numbers are in CLAUDE.md, "Threading, measured".

  THREE HYPOTHESES WERE TESTED AGAINST THE OLD FAILURE AND ARE ALL DEAD, so do
  not re-test them if a new one appears: a METIS symbol collision with MFEM's
  -lmetis; an interaction with MFEM's link line; and something particular to
  meq's matrix -- a plain 5-point Laplacian failed identically.
================================================================================
Performance: profile the inside of a solve, then decide which backend applies
Raised 2026-08-26, rewritten 2026-09-01 against the threading campaign. The goal
is to be fast enough that mesh resolution and polynomial degree are chosen for
accuracy and not for patience.
================================================================================

  MUCH OF THIS ENTRY WAS SUPERSEDED BY THE 2026-08-30 THREADING WORK and has
  been cut. CLAUDE.md's "Threading, measured" and "What to do, in order of
  value" carry the numbers. What is kept here is what is still open, and the two
  arguments that decide the rest.

  WHAT IS STILL OPEN, in order of value.

  1. GET ComputeH()'s ELEMENT-LOCAL DENSE LU OFF THREADED MKL. The largest item
     by a wide margin, and it was invisible until the link line was fixed. The
     per-element LUFactors go through LAPACK, so through MKL, on blocks of order
     10-30; at k = 3 that is FORTY TIMES slower with MKL threading on. meq works
     around it with MKL_NUM_THREADS=1 everywhere, which in turn puts PARDISO's
     threaded advantage out of reach, MKL_NUM_THREADS being process-wide. Two
     routes and neither is done: mkl_set_num_threads_local() around the trace
     solve, or LocalFactorMode::Batched for the local factorisations.

  2. PROFILE THE INSIDE OF A NEWTON SOLVE. tests/performance/ measures the
     assembly-versus-trace-solve split across two thread axes and four sizes, so
     "nothing has ever been profiled" is no longer true at that granularity.
     What is missing is the breakdown WITHIN a solve -- residual against
     gradient against recovery -- and the reconstruction. perf, or
     MFEM_USE_CALIPER.

  3. ELEMENT-LOCAL PARALLELISM, WHICH IS UPSTREAM'S.
     ../mfem-hdg-dev/doc/HDG-ELEMENT-LOCAL-PARALLELISM.md maps all twelve
     sequential element loops in darcyhybridization.cpp, separates the two
     hazards -- loop-hoisted scratch, which is easy, from write conflicts on
     trace dofs shared between adjacent elements, which is not -- and names two
     pieces MFEM already has: Mesh::GetElementColoring for a conflict-free
     schedule, and linalg/batched/, whose BatchedLinAlg::LUFactor and LUSolve
     carry native, cuBLAS and MAGMA backends. InvertA() collapses to a single
     batched call with no data movement, DenseTensor's non-owning constructor
     matching the existing offset layout.

     NPC IS WHAT MAKES THAT A GOOD BATCHED WORKLOAD, and it is meq's default
     now: the per-element work is a LINEAR solve of fixed size rather than a
     nonlinear one of unpredictable iteration count, and
     GetNumLocalNLIterations() reads exactly zero. Design the batching against
     that, not against the nonlinear local solves this entry was first written
     for.

     PART OF IT HAS LANDED, and it is worth about 1.2x sometimes and a 1.8x LOSS
     otherwise. DarcyHybridization::SetAssemblyMode(Threaded), reached through
     meq's setAssemblyMode(), threads the element-local half of ComputeH()
     bit-for-bit against serial. It is NOT the default: HighBetaConvergence,
     which assembles a small system hundreds of times inside a bordered Newton,
     goes 21.5 s -> 39 s with it on. Mesh size does not separate the good case
     from the bad one; how OFTEN assembly is called does, and the solver cannot
     know which caller it has.

  4. PARDISO'S REPRODUCIBILITY -- the entry above.

  5. MEASURE LSsolveType ACROSS THE SUITE rather than on one case. LOWER VALUE
     THAN IT WAS, and possibly zero: under NPC there is no element-local
     nonlinear solve at all, so the local solver type, its preconditioner, its
     iteration cap and its tolerances are all inert. It bears only on
     CondenseThenLinearise, which is the backup. Measured once, on section 4.2
     at k = 1, h = 0.05: Newton 42 outer iterations, LBFGS 36, LBB 25.

  TWO ARGUMENTS THAT DECIDE THE REST, AND NEITHER HAS CHANGED.

  PARTIAL ASSEMBLY WANTS TENSOR-PRODUCT ELEMENTS AND meq USES TRIANGLES. There
  are no AssemblePA or AddMultPA methods anywhere in fem/darcy/, so asking for
  AssemblyLevel::PARTIAL gets an unimplemented path rather than a fast one, and
  writing those kernels is upstream work. But the performance case for PA is sum
  factorisation, which wants quads in 2D, and meq meshes are triangles
  throughout because that is what the GS papers use and what the extension
  technique's transfer paths were measured on. A serious PA effort has to answer
  the quad question first, and that is a discretisation decision with its own
  convergence consequences, not a build flag.

  THIS MACHINE CAN DEVELOP A DEVICE PATH AND MUST NOT DECIDE IT. MFEM_USE_CUDA
  and MFEM_USE_CUDSS are both YES now, so the whole path can be written and
  debugged locally -- and cuDSS has been: mfem::CuDSSSolver agrees with UMFPACK
  to 3.5e-14 from 9,408 to 148,224 trace dofs. Its TIMINGS here vary by a factor
  of THIRTY run to run at fixed size, because WSL2 shares the GPU with the
  Windows host and a consumer card runs FP64 at 1/32 of FP32 where a datacentre
  part runs it at about 1/2. That is two orders of magnitude in the ratio and it
  INVERTS the conclusion: a cuDSS timing that loses to UMFPACK here says nothing
  about an A100 or an MI250X. Correctness answers taken here are portable;
  timings are not, and a local timing must never be the reason a device path is
  abandoned. TIME IT ON A DATACENTRE PART.

  (Also learned, and now guarded in the harness: a device timing taken without
  cudaDeviceSynchronize() reads 2.0e-04 s for a 148k-unknown factorisation --
  four orders out, and plausible enough to publish.)

  WHAT MFEM WRAPS FOR THE TRACE SYSTEM, and whether it applies here. cuDSS is
  the best fit and is already run: serial constructor, so no MPI, and
  SetReorderingReuse. MKL PARDISO is the only other one meq has run. AmgX wants
  a symmetric definite operator, which the trace matrix is ONLY on the fitted
  path -- on the extension path its relative asymmetry is 0.54 -- so it is a
  fitted-path option at best, and serial MFEM has no AMG anyway. SuperLU_DIST,
  MUMPS and STRUMPACK are distributed solvers whose point is MPI, and meq is
  serial by design.

  AND A CHEAP CHECK NOBODY HAS DONE: confirm the release build is what everyone
  thinks it is. meq builds -O3 -DNDEBUG; MFEM's install carries
  MFEM_USE_MEMALLOC = YES, whose stack-based allocator is a help or a hindrance
  depending on the access pattern and has never been measured either way here.

================================================================================
Stale comments and inert keys, found by writing the LaTeX manual
Raised 2026-09-03.
================================================================================

  Found by writing docs/manual/ against the HEADERS rather than against the
  docs. That is a different reading of the same tree, which is why these
  surfaced now and not during the Sphinx pass.

  src/meq/meq.hpp COVERS 5 OF 17 HEADERS AND ITS COMMENT IS FALSE. It still
  says Estimator.hpp does not compile against MFEM 4.5.1, which stopped being
  true at stage 6. A consumer who includes the umbrella header gets less than
  half the library and is told nothing about it. Either complete it or say in
  it that it is deliberately a subset -- the present state is the worst of the
  three, because it looks complete.

  Estimator.hpp's file comment says postProcess() refuses the semi-linear path,
  so the estimator is linear-path only. That refusal was removed and the
  adaptive loop runs on the non-linear path in production.

  Config.hpp's header comment says "six tables". There are eight.

  LinearMaxIterations and LinearTolerance are parsed, validated, and consumed
  by NOTHING. Either wire them to the fallback Krylov path or refuse them; a
  key that validates and does nothing is worse than one that is rejected.

  PPrimeVariable, PPrimeFit, GGPrimeVariable and GGPrimeFit are REFUSED on the
  rotating path and SILENTLY ACCEPTED AND IGNORED on the mhd path. The same
  keys, two behaviours, and the quiet one is the wrong one.

  And the project's own name has never been settled: CLAUDE.md writes it lower
  case throughout, README.md writes MEQ fifteen times, and docs/manual/
  renders \meq as \textsc{MEQ}. Cosmetic, and one token in Manual.tex.
